Note: This specification is under active development and therefore incomplete. If you're looking for an overview of the proposal, please refer to the [Explainer](https://github.com/WICG/local-peer-to-peer/blob/main/EXPLAINER.md).

Introduction {#intro}
=====================

*This section is non-normative.*

The Local Peer-to-Peer API aims to give browsers the means to communicate directly, without the aid of a server in the middle. It is designed to enable this communication within the confines of a [=local communication medium=].

Many modern Web security measures rely on the presence of naming, signaling and certificate authorities. Local use-cases where these authorities are not readily available have started lagging behind in user experience or are not supported altogether. The Local Peer-to-Peer API aims to bring back first-class support for local communication use-cases while working within the same strict user-friendliness, security and privacy requirements.

Examples of potential uses of this API include: Collaboration tools that work during an internet outage or emergency situations, connecting to your NAS, your home security system, your robotic assistant doing the dishes or your GPU farm in the basement that's running your personalized virtual assistant. See also [Use Cases](https://github.com/WICG/local-peer-to-peer/blob/main/EXPLAINER.md#use-cases).

This specification aims to strike a balance between creating a powerful new building block for developers and providing a seamless, secure and privacy preserving experience for browser users. As an example: while the API doesn't provide raw socket access, it does aim to give developers the flexibility to innovate on top by providing a persistent, two-way communication channel with little overhead.

The API is designed to be backed by an authenticated, streams-based transport. As a commitment to an open standards-based implementation path, this specification describes how the API can be implemented on top of the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]. While not described here, the API is expected to be implementable on top of other transports when technically feasible.

Terminology {#terminology}
======================================================

The term <dfn>local communication medium</dfn> refers to a communication medium that provides a local, direct communication channel between the communicating devices. This is a purposefully broad term that covers any medium that can be used to establish an authenticated, streams-based transport between the devices. Examples may include but are not limited to: a wired or wireless local area network, a Thread network, Bluetooth Low Energy or Wi-Fi Direct. This spec will not mandate which underlying technologies must be supported.

The term <dfn>local communication</dfn> refers to data communication over the [=local communication medium=].

The term <dfn>communication medium topology</dfn> refers to the composition of the [=local communication medium=] which may include sensitive information such as IP or other address information or the number of devices connected to the medium.

Permission Policy Integration {#permission-policy-integration}
======================================================

The Local Peer-to-Peer API defines a [=policy-controlled feature=] identified by the token "local-peer-to-peer". Its [=default allowlist=] is `"self"`.

Workers (dedicated and shared) adhere to the permission policy set by their owning document(s):
<ul>
  <li>
    Dedicated workers can be created from other workers, in which case the permission policy of the first owning document (in case of a dedicated worker) or owning documents (in case of a shared worker) up the owner chain will be used.
  </li>
  <li>
    Shared workers often have multiple owning documents as they can be obtained by other documents with the [=same origin=]. In this case, all owning documents must be [=allowed to use=] the [=policy-controlled feature=] defined by this specification.
  </li>
</ul>

Note: There has been discussion on allowing setting permission policy directly on a worker on creation, in which case that would have to be consulted as well.
 
The [=policy-controlled feature/default allowlist=] of `"self"` allows usage in same-origin nested frames but prevents third-party content from using the feature. Third-party content usage can be selectively enabled by adding `allow="local-peer-to-peer"` attribute to the frame container element:

<pre class="example" title= "Enabling Local Peer-to-Peer API on third-party content" highlight='js'>
&lt;iframe src="https://third-party.com" allow="local-peer-to-peer"/&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
</pre>

Alternatively, the Local Peer-to-Peer API can be disabled completely by specifying the permissions policy in a HTTP response header:

<pre class="example" title="Permission Policy over HTTP" highlight='js'>
Permissions-Policy: local-peer-to-peer=()
</pre>

See [[!PERMISSIONS-POLICY]] for more details.

Peer Management {#peer-management}
==================================

Note: This section and its subsections use RFC 2119 terminology in a relaxed manner. These sections will be converted into well-defined algorithmic normative prose informed by further implementation experience. For now, this relaxed description better allows for rapid prototyping.

The user agent is in charge of managing peers.

A <dfn>peer</dfn> is an equal participant in the [=local communication=]. These nodes can communicate without the need for a central coordination by a server.

A user agent has an associated <dfn>local peer-to-peer manager</dfn> in charge of managing [=peers=]. Its responsibility is to [=start local peer discovery=], [=establish local peer connections=] and [=acquire a local peer grant=] on a per-origin basis. This is done to avoid exposing information about the [=communication medium topology=].

Known peers {#known-peers}
--------------------------

The user agent should maintain a list of peers that it has knowledge of.

The [=local peer-to-peer manager=] has an associated <dfn>known peers map</dfn>, an [=/ordered map=] of [=known peers=]. Each <dfn>known peer</dfn> is a [=peer=] that has an associated <dfn>authentication state</dfn> and <dfn>peer grant state</dfn>. The user agent keeps track of the [=authentication state=] per peer and the [=peer grant state=] per peer, per origin. Unless [=persisted=], both the states are initially false.

Note: The [=peer grant state=] is origin-level while the [=authentication state=] is peer-level. The rationale is to not require the user to undergo authentication multiple times between the same peers. This design may change informed by security and privacy considerations.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#24): Validate whether peer-level [=authentication state=] is appropriate.

The user agent may persist known peers, their [=authentication states=] and/or [=peer grant states=]. If the user agent chooses to do so, it must do so in accordance with the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] [Persistent State rules](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#persistent-state). Such a peer or its state is said to be <dfn>persisted</dfn>.

Peer advertisement {#peer-advertisement}
----------------------------------------

The user agent can make itself discoverable by advertising using the [=session/advertise agent=] capability.

Note: The user agent must get the user's explicit consent in order to start advertising.

When advertising, the user agent must listen for incoming peer connections. In case a connection is received, the peer is added to the [=known peers map=]. The user agent must not directly provide access to the incoming peer connection to any origin. Instead, the user agent must prompt to [grant peer](#peer-grant) access to the origin that initiated peer advertisement.

Peer discovery {#peer-discovery}
--------------------------------

The user agent can discover local peers using the [=session/discover agents=] capability.

When asked to <dfn>start local peer discovery</dfn>, the user agent discovers local peers using the [=session/discover agents=] capability.

If a peer is discovered, the user agent should add it to the [=known peers map=]. The user agent must never expose the full result of peer discovery with an origin. Instead, the user agent must [=acquire a local peer grant=] to grant access to a peer for the origin that initiated the peer request.

Peer authentication {#peer-authentication}
------------------------------------------

The user agent can initiate authentication with a peer using the [=session/authenticate an agent=] capability.

When asked to <dfn>authenticate a local peer</dfn>, the user agent must initiate authentication with a local peer using the [=session/authenticate an agent=] capability. The peer's [=authentication state=] must be set to true if authentication succeeds, otherwise false.

A peer is said to be <dfn>authenticated</dfn> when its [=authentication state=] is true.

Peer grant {#peer-grant}
------------------------------------

The user agent, with the user's consent, must [=acquire a local peer grant=] for an origin to get access to a peer.

Note: The user agent must get the user's explicit consent in order to grant an origin access to a peer. For enhanced privacy protection, the user agent must provide means to dismiss any related user interface and this action must not be detectable by script or have script-observable side-effects. The user interface may provide means to revoke the grant or to make the grant [=persisted=].

When asked to <dfn>acquire a local peer grant</dfn>, the user agent displays the [=known peers=] to the user through its native user interface. When the user selects a peer from the user interface, the user agent must check the selected peer is [=authenticated=], and run the [=authenticate a local peer=] algorithm otherwise, and set the [=peer grant state=] according to the user's explicit selection.

Protocol concepts {#protocol-concepts}
======================================

When asked to <dfn>establish local peer connection</dfn>, the user agent establishes a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] with the peer.

Issue: Define the steps to establish local peer connection.

A <dfn for="protocol">Local Peer-to-Peer session</dfn> represents an authenticated [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] connection as defined in [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] or OSP.

A [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] has the following capabilities:

<table class="data" dfn-for="session">
 <thead>
  <tr>
   <th>capability
   <th>definition
  </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
  <tr>
   <td><dfn>advertise agent</dfn>
   <td>[[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]
   [discovery](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#discovery)
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><dfn>discover agents</dfn>
   <td>[[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]
   [discovery](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#discovery)
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><dfn>discover metadata</dfn>
   <td>[[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]
   [transport](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#transport)
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><dfn>authenticate an agent</dfn>
   <td>[[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]
   [authentication](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#authentication)
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=open a data channel=]
   <td>[[#data-channel-extension]]
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=send data on a channel=]
   <td>[[#data-channel-extension]]
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=close a data channel=]
   <td>[[#data-channel-extension]]
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=open a WebTransport session=]
   <td>[[#webtransport-extension]]
  </tr>
 </tbody>
</table>

Part of these capabilities are defined below as [protocol extensions](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#protocol-extensions) to the [[!openscreen-application|Open Screen Application Protocol]], which runs on the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]].

Data channel extension {#data-channel-extension}
------------------------------------------------

In order to signal support for this protocol extension, the agent should include the [=data-channels=] [agent-capability](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a) as part of the [agent-info-response](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#agent-info-response) message exchanged during [=discover metadata=].

Each data channel uses its own bidirectional [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] stream. All messages of a data channel, in both directions, are sent on that stream, in order. This differs from other Open Screen Protocol messages, which use unidirectional streams.

Note: [w3c/openscreenprotocol#370](https://github.com/w3c/openscreenprotocol/pull/370) describes message delivery as ordered message sequences, independent of QUIC. Each direction of a data channel's stream is one such sequence.

To <dfn>open a data channel</dfn> an agent opens a new bidirectional [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] stream and sends a [=data-channel-open-request=] message as the first message on it. The [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] must be authenticated. The message must contain the following values:

: channel-id
:: An ID number between 0 and 65,534 which uniquely identifies the data channel. The value 65,535 is reserved, as for {{RTCDataChannel}}; {{LP2PDataChannel/id}} is an `unsigned short` for parity with {{RTCDataChannel/id}}, but cannot take that value.

: label
:: a string that contains a name describing the data channel. These labels are not required to be unique.

: protocol
:: a string containing the name of the subprotocol in use. If no protocol was specified when the data channel was created, then this property's value is the empty string ("").

The agent that is the QUIC client of the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] uses even channel IDs and the agent that is the QUIC server uses odd channel IDs, so the two agents never pick the same ID. If the application does not choose an ID, the agent uses the lowest unused ID with its parity. A channel ID is in use from when the [=data-channel-open-request=] is sent or received until the channel is closed. An agent must not open a data channel with an ID that is in use or that has the other agent's parity.

Note: In the API, the requesting peer is the QUIC client and therefore uses even IDs; the receiving peer uses odd IDs.

When the receiver receives the [=data-channel-open-request=], it must send back a [=data-channel-open-response=] message on the same stream, as the first message it sends on that stream. The response must include the following:

: result
:: a code indicating success or failure, and the reason for the failure. The values are those of the `result` type in the [[!openscreen-application|Open Screen Application Protocol]] [Messages](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a).

The receiver must refuse the data channel if the channel-id is greater than 65,534, is in use, or has the receiver's own parity. To refuse a data channel, the receiver sends a [=data-channel-open-response=] with a result other than `success`, which should be `permanent-error` (102), and then closes the stream.

The opening agent must wait for the [=data-channel-open-response=] before it sends any data on the stream. If the result is not `success`, the data channel fails to open and the opening agent closes the stream.

If the [=data-channel-open-response=] message indicates success, the data channel is considered open. Agents can now <dfn>send data on a channel</dfn> by sending [data-frame](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#data-frame) messages (type key 24, as defined in the [[!openscreen-application|Open Screen Application Protocol]]) on the same [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] stream the data channel was opened. The message must include the following keys; the other keys of [data-frame](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#data-frame) (1, 2, 3 and 5) are unused, should not be sent, and must be ignored by the receiving agent:

: encoding-id
:: Determines the encoding of the data being sent. The values are specified as [=data-channel-encoding-id=], instead of referencing a data encoding offer:
    0: [=encoding-id-blob|Blob=];
    1: [=encoding-id-string|String=];
    2: [=encoding-id-array-buffer|ArrayBuffer=].

: payload
:: The binary representation of the data being sent.

To <dfn>close a data channel</dfn>, an agent finishes sending any pending messages and then ends its sending side of the stream (a QUIC STREAM frame with the FIN bit). When an agent receives the end of the stream, it considers the data channel closing, and ends its own sending side once its pending messages are sent. The data channel is closed when both sides of the stream have ended. An agent may instead abort a data channel by resetting the stream (RESET_STREAM and STOP_SENDING); the data channel is then closed at once, and messages not yet delivered may be lost. When the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] is closed, all of its data channels are closed.

WebTransport extension {#webtransport-extension}
------------------------------------------------

The protocol extension to [=send data on a channel=] provides an ergonomic way to send simple messages. An agent can [=open a WebTransport session=] for communication that requires low overhead and more granular streams control.

In order to signal support for this protocol extension, the agent should include the [=quic-transport=] [agent-capability](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a) as part of the [agent-info-response](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#agent-info-response) message exchanged during [=discover metadata=].

An agent can <dfn>open a WebTransport session</dfn> by dialing a new [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] connection using the agent certificates established during [=authenticate an agent=]. During connection establishment, the ALPN token "q2q" must be used in the TLS handshake.

The capabilities of the Local WebTransport session are defined in [[!webtransport]].

Note: The WebTransport-over-QUIC protocol is yet to be defined. Potentially considering earlier work such as [draft-vvv-webtransport-quic](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-vvv-webtransport-quic-02).

Local HTTPS {#local-https}
==========================

Authentication establishes a mutual trust anchor between peers on the [=local communication medium=]. This trust anchor can also be used to validate certificates used by HTTP servers on the [=local communication medium=]. An Open Screen Network Protocol agent certificate can be identified by the common name of its subject ending in "._openscreen._udp". When a user agent loads a webpage and finds an agent certificate in the certificate chain, it can use it as a trust anchor for authentication of the server. If the corresponding agent is not [=authenticated=], the user agent should prompt the user to authenticate the agent before proceeding with certificate validation.

Endpoint discovery {#local-https-discovery}
-------------------------------------------
If a peer is [=authenticated=], it may advertize one or more HTTPS endpoints it provides. A user agent may display these to the user to connect to servers on the [=local communication medium=] without knowledge of the corresponding hostname and/or IP address.

Issue: define endpoint-info messages

<section algorithm="LP2PReceiver">

LP2PReceiver Interface {#lp2p-receiver}
================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PReceiver</dfn> interface allows advertising on the [=local communication medium=], enabling other peers to discover and connect.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PReceiver : EventTarget {
  constructor(optional LP2PReceiverOptions options = {});
  attribute EventHandler onconnection;
  Promise&lt;undefined&gt; start();
  undefined stop();
};
</pre>

Each {{LP2PReceiver}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[StartPromise]]</dfn>, a {{Promise}} or null, initially null, and a <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[State]]</dfn>, one of "`initial`", "`advertising`" or "`stopped`", initially "`initial`".

An {{LP2PReceiver}} is used once: after {{LP2PReceiver/stop()}} it cannot be started again.

<div algorithm="LP2PReceiver constructor">
The <dfn constructor for="LP2PReceiver">constructor(|options|)</dfn> steps are:

1. Set [=this=]'s <dfn for="LP2PReceiver">nickname</dfn> to |options|["{{LP2PReceiverOptions/nickname}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or the empty string otherwise.

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PReceiver">start()</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} is not null, return it.
1. If [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", return [=a promise rejected with=] an "{{InvalidStateError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is not permitted to use the "local-peer-to-peer" [=policy-controlled feature=], as described in [[#permission-policy-integration]], return [=a promise rejected with=] a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |promise| be [=a new promise=].
1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} to |promise|.
1. Let |global| be [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=].
1. Run these steps [=in parallel=]:
    1. Ask the user for permission to advertise [=this=]'s [=LP2PReceiver/nickname=] on the [=local communication medium=] on behalf of |global|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=], as described in [[#peer-advertisement]].
    1. If the user did not grant permission, or the user agent does not allow advertising, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to set [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} to null and [=reject=] |promise| with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}, and abort these steps.
    1. If [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", abort these steps.
    1. Start advertising using the [=session/advertise agent=] capability.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to run these steps:
        1. If [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", abort these steps.
        1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} to "`advertising`".
        1. [=Resolve=] |promise| with undefined.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

Note: Whether the user declined to advertise or dismissed the user interface, the user agent takes the same steps, so that script cannot tell the two apart. See [[#peer-grant]].

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PReceiver">stop()</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", return.
1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} to "`stopped`".
1. If [=this=].{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} is not null and is still pending, [=reject=] it with an "{{AbortError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Stop advertising, if advertising was started.

</div>

Stopping does not affect the {{LP2PConnection}} objects that were already received; their [=Local Peer-to-Peer sessions=] stay open until closed.

<div algorithm="LP2PReceiver incoming session">
While an {{LP2PReceiver}} |receiver| is advertising, when a peer establishes a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] |session| with the user agent, the user agent must run these steps:

1. Let |global| be |receiver|'s [=relevant global object=].
1. Let |origin| be |global|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=].
1. Add the peer to the [=known peers map=], and obtain the user's consent to grant the peer access to |origin|, as described in [[#peer-advertisement]] and [[#peer-grant]]. If the peer is not [=authenticated=], this includes running the [=authenticate a local peer=] steps.
1. If the peer's [=peer grant state=] for |origin| is false, close |session| and abort these steps.
1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to run these steps:
    1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is not "`advertising`", close |session| and abort these steps.
    1. Let |connection| be the result of [=creating an LP2PConnection=] given |session| and |global|.
    1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PReceiver/connection}} at |receiver| using {{LP2PConnectionEvent}} with its {{LP2PConnectionEvent/connection}} attribute initialized to |connection|.

</div>

LP2PReceiverOptions {#lp2p-receiver-options}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PReceiverOptions {
    DOMString nickname;
};
</pre>

LP2PConnectionEvent {#lp2p-receiver-connection-event}
----------------------------------------------------

Issue: In general, when defining a new interface that inherits from Event please always ask feedback from the WHATWG or the W3C WebApps WG community. See [defining event interfaces](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#defining-event-interfaces).

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PConnectionEvent : Event {
    constructor(DOMString type, LP2PConnectionEventInit connectionEventInitDict);
    readonly attribute LP2PConnection connection;
};

dictionary LP2PConnectionEventInit : EventInit {
    required LP2PConnection connection;
};
</pre>

Events {#lp2p-receiver-events}
------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PReceiver}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PReceiver"><code>connection</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PConnectionEvent}}</td>
      <td>An incoming connection is received.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Event handlers {#lp2p-receiver-event-handlers}
----------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the [[#lp2p-receiver|LP2PReceiver]] interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver"><code>onconnection</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PReceiver/connection}}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Examples {#lp2p-receiver-examples}
---------------------------------

Example: Setting up a receiver to listen for connections:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});
receiver.onconnection = e => {
    console.log("Connection established!");
    const conn = e.connection;
};

// Blocks until permission is received.
await receiver.start();
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PRequest">

The LP2PRequest Interface {#lp2p-request}
================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PRequest</dfn> interface represents a request for a connection to another local peer.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PRequest {
    constructor(optional LP2PRequestOptions options = {});
    Promise&lt;LP2PConnection&gt; start();
};
</pre>

Each {{LP2PRequest}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PRequest">\[[StartPromise]]</dfn>, a {{Promise}} or null, initially null.

<div algorithm="LP2PRequest constructor">
The <dfn constructor for="LP2PRequest">constructor(|options|)</dfn> steps are:

1. Set [=this=]'s <dfn for="LP2PRequest">nickname</dfn> to |options|["{{LP2PRequestOptions/nickname}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or the empty string otherwise.

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PRequest">start()</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} is not null, return it.
1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is not permitted to use the "local-peer-to-peer" [=policy-controlled feature=], as described in [[#permission-policy-integration]], return [=a promise rejected with=] a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |promise| be [=a new promise=].
1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} to |promise|.
1. Let |global| be [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=].
1. Let |origin| be |global|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=].
1. Run these steps [=in parallel=]:
    1. [=Start local peer discovery=].
    1. [=Acquire a local peer grant=] for |origin|, presenting [=this=]'s [=LP2PRequest/nickname=] to the user. Let |peer| be the [=known peer=] the user selected, or null if the user did not select a peer, declined, or dismissed the user interface.
    1. If |peer| is null, or |peer|'s [=peer grant state=] for |origin| is false, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to set [=this=].{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} to null and [=reject=] |promise| with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}, and abort these steps.
    1. [=Establish local peer connection=] with |peer|, and let |session| be the resulting [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=].
    1. If establishing the connection failed, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to set [=this=].{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} to null and [=reject=] |promise| with a "{{NetworkError}}" {{DOMException}}, and abort these steps.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to run these steps:
        1. Let |connection| be the result of [=creating an LP2PConnection=] given |session| and |global|.
        1. [=Resolve=] |promise| with |connection|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

Calling {{LP2PRequest/start()}} again while the request is pending, or after it succeeded, returns the same promise. After a rejection, {{LP2PRequest/start()}} can be called again to start over.

Note: Whether the user declined, dismissed the user interface, or selected a peer that could not be [=authenticated=], the user agent takes the same steps, so that script cannot tell these apart. See [[#peer-grant]].

LP2PRequestOptions {#lp2p-request-options}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PRequestOptions {
    DOMString nickname;
};
</pre>

Examples {#lp2p-request-examples}
---------------------------------

Example: Setting up a request for a connection:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

// Blocks until connection is received.
const conn = await request.start();
console.log("Connection established!");
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PConnection">

The LP2PConnection Interface {#lp2p-connection}
===============================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PConnection</dfn> interface represents a connection with another local peer.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PConnection : EventTarget {
  undefined close();
  attribute EventHandler onclose;
};
</pre>

Each {{LP2PConnection}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection">\[[Session]]</dfn>, a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], and a <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection">\[[Closed]]</dfn>, a boolean, initially false.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create an LP2PConnection</dfn> given a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] |session| and a [=global object=] |global|:

1. Let |connection| be a [=new=] {{LP2PConnection}} in |global|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. Set |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} to |session|.
1. Return |connection|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PConnection">close()</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, return.
1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} to true.
1. Close [=this=].{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}. This closes all of its data channels, as described in [=close a data channel=].
1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] to [=fire an event=] named {{LP2PConnection/close}} at [=this=].

</div>

<div algorithm="LP2PConnection session closed">
When the user agent learns that the {{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} of an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection| has been closed, whether by the peer or because the underlying transport failed, it must [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |connection|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, abort these steps.
1. Set |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} to true.
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PConnection/close}} at |connection|.

</div>

The {{LP2PConnection/close}} event is fired at most once for a given {{LP2PConnection}}.

Events {#lp2p-connection-events}
--------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PConnection}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PConnection"><code>close</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The connection is closed, by {{LP2PConnection/close()}} or by the peer.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Event handlers {#lp2p-connection-event-handlers}
------------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the {{LP2PConnection}} interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection"><code>onclose</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PConnection/close}}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

LP2PQuicTransport Interface Extensions {#lp2p-connection-quic-transport-extensions}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

This {{LP2PQuicTransport}} extension allows opening a QuicTransport using an existing connection. In this case, the already established permission and grants should be used.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
partial interface LP2PQuicTransport {
  constructor(LP2PConnection connection,
              optional LP2PQuicTransportInit quicTransportDict = {});
};
</pre>

Examples {#lp2p-connection-examples}
----------------------------------------

Example: Open a {{LP2PQuicTransport}} using an existing connection as receiver:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});
receiver.onconnection = async e => {
    const conn = e.connection;
    
    const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(conn);

    // Blocks until transport is ready.
    await transport.ready;
};

// Blocks until permission is received.
await receiver.start();
</pre>

Example: Open a {{LP2PQuicTransport}} using an existing connection as requester:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

// Blocks until connection is received.
const conn = await request.start();

const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(conn);

// Blocks until transport is ready.
await transport.ready;
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PDataChannel">

The LP2PDataChannel Interface {#lp2p-data-channel}
==========================================

The LP2PDataChannel interface represents a bi-directional data channel between two peers. An LP2PDataChannel is created via a [factory method](#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions) on a LP2PConnection object.

Note: The LP2PDataChannel interface is purposefully kept as close as possible to the [RTCDataChannel](https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/#rtcdatachannel) interface defined in [[!webrtc]]. The aim is to allow seamless transition of developers that are familiar with WebRTC as well as allowing libraries to easily work with both the [[!webrtc]] and LP2P API.

<pre class="idl">
enum LP2PDataChannelState {
  "connecting",
  "open",
  "closing",
  "closed"
};

[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PDataChannel : EventTarget {
  readonly attribute USVString label;
  readonly attribute USVString protocol;
  readonly attribute unsigned short? id;
  readonly attribute LP2PDataChannelState readyState;
  readonly attribute unsigned long long bufferedAmount;
  attribute unsigned long long bufferedAmountLowThreshold;
  attribute EventHandler onopen;
  attribute EventHandler onbufferedamountlow;
  attribute EventHandler onerror;
  attribute EventHandler onclosing;
  attribute EventHandler onclose;
  undefined close();
  attribute EventHandler onmessage;
  attribute BinaryType binaryType;
  undefined send((USVString or Blob or ArrayBuffer or ArrayBufferView) data);
};
</pre>

The {{LP2PDataChannelState}} values have the same meaning as those of {{RTCDataChannelState}}: "{{LP2PDataChannelState/connecting}}" while the channel is being opened, "{{LP2PDataChannelState/open}}" once it can be used, "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closing}}" while it is being closed, and "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closed}}" once it is closed.

Each {{LP2PDataChannel}} has an <dfn for="LP2PDataChannel">underlying data channel</dfn>, a data channel of a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] as described in [[#data-channel-extension]], and the following internal slots:

- <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[Label]]</dfn>, <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[Protocol]]</dfn> and <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[Id]]</dfn>, the label, protocol and channel ID of the [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=];
- <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[ReadyState]]</dfn>, an {{LP2PDataChannelState}}, initially "{{LP2PDataChannelState/connecting}}";
- <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[BufferedAmount]]</dfn>, a number, initially 0;
- <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[BufferedAmountLowThreshold]]</dfn>, a number, initially 0;
- <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">\[[BinaryType]]</dfn>, a {{BinaryType}}, initially "{{BinaryType/arraybuffer}}".

The <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">label</dfn>, <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">protocol</dfn>, <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">id</dfn>, <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">readyState</dfn> and <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">bufferedAmount</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[Label]]}}, [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[Protocol]]}}, [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[Id]]}}, [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} and [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmount]]}}, respectively.

The {{LP2PDataChannel/bufferedAmount}} attribute is the number of bytes of application data (text and binary data, without framing) that have been queued with {{LP2PDataChannel/send()}} but have not yet been sent on the [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=]. It does not reset to zero when the channel closes.

The <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">bufferedAmountLowThreshold</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmountLowThreshold]]}}, and its setter steps are to set [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmountLowThreshold]]}} to [=the given value=].

The <dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel">binaryType</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BinaryType]]}}, and its setter steps are to set [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BinaryType]]}} to [=the given value=]. It controls how binary messages are exposed: with "{{BinaryType/blob}}" they are delivered as {{Blob}} objects, with "{{BinaryType/arraybuffer}}" as {{ArrayBuffer}} objects. The receiver's {{LP2PDataChannel/binaryType}} alone decides this: whether the sender passed a {{Blob}} ([=encoding-id-blob=]) or an {{ArrayBuffer}} ([=encoding-id-array-buffer=]) to {{LP2PDataChannel/send()}} makes no difference.

Note: The default of "{{BinaryType/arraybuffer}}" matches {{RTCDataChannel/binaryType}}, not {{WebSocket/binaryType}}.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create an LP2PDataChannel</dfn> given an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection|, a string |label|, a string |protocol| and a channel ID |id|:

1. Let |channel| be a [=new=] {{LP2PDataChannel}} in |connection|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. Set |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[Label]]}} to |label|, |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[Protocol]]}} to |protocol| and |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[Id]]}} to |id|.
1. Let |channel|'s [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=] be the data channel with channel ID |id| of |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}.
1. Return |channel|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>announce an LP2PDataChannel as open</dfn> given |channel|, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |channel|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. If |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} is not "{{LP2PDataChannelState/connecting}}", abort these steps.
1. Set |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} to "{{LP2PDataChannelState/open}}".
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/open}} at |channel|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>announce an LP2PDataChannel as closing</dfn> given |channel|, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |channel|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. If |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} is "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closing}}" or "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closed}}", abort these steps.
1. Set |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} to "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closing}}".
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/closing}} at |channel|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>announce an LP2PDataChannel as closed</dfn> given |channel|, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |channel|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. If |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} is "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closed}}", abort these steps.
1. Set |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} to "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closed}}".
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/close}} at |channel|.

</div>

<div algorithm="LP2PDataChannel underlying data channel events">
For an {{LP2PDataChannel}} |channel|, the user agent must run the following steps as the state of its [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=] changes, as described in [[#data-channel-extension]]:

- When the [=data-channel-open-response=] for a channel opened by {{LP2PConnection/createDataChannel()}} indicates success, [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as open=] given |channel|.
- When the [=data-channel-open-response=] indicates failure, or the channel is aborted before it is open, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |channel|'s [=relevant global object=] to [=fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/error}} at |channel|, and then [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as closed=] given |channel|.
- When the peer ends its sending side of the channel's stream, [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as closing=] given |channel|.
- When the [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=] is closed, for whatever reason, [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as closed=] given |channel|.

</div>

The {{LP2PDataChannel/closing}} event is thus only fired when the peer closed the channel, as for {{RTCDataChannel}}; {{LP2PDataChannel/close()}} sets {{LP2PDataChannel/readyState}} to "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closing}}" without firing it.

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PDataChannel">send(|data|)</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} is not "{{LP2PDataChannelState/open}}", [=exception/throw=] an "{{InvalidStateError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |encoding| and |payload| be determined as follows:
    : |data| is a string
    :: Let |encoding| be [=encoding-id-string=] and |payload| be the result of [=UTF-8 encoding=] |data|.
    : |data| is an {{ArrayBuffer}} or {{ArrayBufferView}}
    :: Let |encoding| be [=encoding-id-array-buffer=] and |payload| be [=get a copy of the buffer source|a copy of the bytes held by=] |data|.
    : |data| is a {{Blob}}
    :: Let |encoding| be [=encoding-id-blob=] and |payload| be the raw data represented by |data|. The data is read asynchronously; messages queued after it are not sent before it.
1. Increase [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmount]]}} by |payload|'s [=byte sequence/length=].
1. Run these steps [=in parallel=]:
    1. [=Send data on a channel|Send=] |payload| with encoding-id |encoding| on [=this=]'s [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=], after any messages queued earlier.
    1. Once |payload| has been sent, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:
        1. Let |previous| be [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmount]]}}.
        1. Decrease [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmount]]}} by |payload|'s [=byte sequence/length=].
        1. If |previous| is greater than [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmountLowThreshold]]}} and [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BufferedAmount]]}} is less than or equal to it, [=fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/bufferedamountlow}} at [=this=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PDataChannel">close()</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} is "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closing}}" or "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closed}}", return.
1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} to "{{LP2PDataChannelState/closing}}".
1. [=Close a data channel|Close=] [=this=]'s [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=] [=in parallel=]. Messages queued with {{LP2PDataChannel/send()}} are sent first.

</div>

Once the [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=] is closed, the user agent [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as closed|announces the channel as closed=], as described above.

<div algorithm="LP2PDataChannel receive a message">
When a [data-frame](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#data-frame) message with encoding-id |encoding| and payload |payload| is received on the [=LP2PDataChannel/underlying data channel=] of an {{LP2PDataChannel}} |channel|, the user agent must [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |channel|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. If |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} is not "{{LP2PDataChannelState/open}}", abort these steps.
1. Let |data| be determined as follows:
    : |encoding| is [=encoding-id-string=]
    :: Let |data| be the result of [=UTF-8 decode without BOM|UTF-8 decoding=] |payload|.
    : |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[BinaryType]]}} is "{{BinaryType/blob}}"
    :: Let |data| be a new {{Blob}} object, created in the [=relevant realm=] of |channel|, that represents |payload| as its raw data.
    : Otherwise
    :: Let |data| be the result of [=ArrayBuffer/create|creating an ArrayBuffer=] from |payload| in the [=relevant realm=] of |channel|.
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/message}} at |channel| using {{MessageEvent}} with its {{MessageEvent/data}} attribute initialized to |data|.

</div>

LP2PDataChannelInit {#lp2p-data-channel-init}
----------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PDataChannelInit {
  USVString protocol = "";
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short id;
};
</pre>

Events {#lp2p-data-channel-events}
---------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PDataChannel}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>open</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The data channel is opened.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>bufferedamountlow</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/bufferedAmount}} decreased from above {{LP2PDataChannel/bufferedAmountLowThreshold}} to at or below it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>message</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{MessageEvent}}[[html]]</td>
      <td>An incoming message is received.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>error</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>An error occurred.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>closing</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The data channel is closing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>close</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The data channel is closed.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Event handlers {#lp2p-data-channel-event-handlers}
---------------------------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the [[#lp2p-data-channel|LP2PDataChannel]] interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onopen</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/open}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onbufferedamountlow</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/bufferedamountlow}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onmessage</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/message}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onerror</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/error}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onclosing</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/closing}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onclose</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/close}}</td>
    </tr>
</table>

LP2PConnection Interface Extensions {#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
partial interface LP2PConnection {
  LP2PDataChannel createDataChannel(USVString label,
                                    optional LP2PDataChannelInit dataChannelDict = {});
  attribute EventHandler ondatachannel;
};
</pre>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PConnection">createDataChannel(|label|, |dataChannelDict|)</dfn> method steps are:

1. If [=this=].{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, [=exception/throw=] an "{{InvalidStateError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |session| be [=this=].{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}.
1. Let |id| be |dataChannelDict|["{{LP2PDataChannelInit/id}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or null otherwise.
1. If |id| is 65535, [=exception/throw=] a {{TypeError}}.
1. If |id| is not null and is in use on |session|, or has the parity of the other agent, as described in [[#data-channel-extension]], [=exception/throw=] an "{{OperationError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. If |id| is null, set |id| to the lowest channel ID with this agent's parity that is not in use on |session|. If there is no such ID, [=exception/throw=] an "{{OperationError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |channel| be the result of [=creating an LP2PDataChannel=] given [=this=], |label|, |dataChannelDict|["{{LP2PDataChannelInit/protocol}}"] and |id|.
1. [=Open a data channel=] on |session| with channel ID |id|, label |label| and protocol |dataChannelDict|["{{LP2PDataChannelInit/protocol}}"], [=in parallel=]. |id| is in use from this point.
1. Return |channel|.

</div>

The channel is [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as open|announced as open=] once the peer accepts it, or [=announce an LP2PDataChannel as closed|announced as closed=], after an {{LP2PDataChannel/error}} event, if the peer refuses it.

<div algorithm="LP2PConnection incoming data channel">
When the user agent receives a [=data-channel-open-request=] on the {{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} of an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection| and accepts it, as described in [[#data-channel-extension]], it must [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |connection|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, abort these steps.
1. Let |channel| be the result of [=creating an LP2PDataChannel=] given |connection| and the label, protocol and channel-id of the request.
1. Set |channel|.{{LP2PDataChannel/[[ReadyState]]}} to "{{LP2PDataChannelState/open}}".
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PConnection/datachannel}} at |connection| using {{LP2PDataChannelEvent}} with its {{LP2PDataChannelEvent/channel}} attribute initialized to |channel|.
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PDataChannel/open}} at |channel|.

</div>

Note: An incoming channel is already open when the {{LP2PConnection/datachannel}} event is fired, so that it can be used from the event handler; the {{LP2PDataChannel/open}} event is fired as well, as for {{RTCDataChannel}}.

LP2PDataChannelEvent {#lp2p-data-channel-event}
------------------------------------------------

Issue: In general, when defining a new interface that inherits from Event please always ask feedback from the WHATWG or the W3C WebApps WG community. See [defining event interfaces](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#defining-event-interfaces).

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PDataChannelEvent : Event {
  constructor(DOMString type, LP2PDataChannelEventInit dataChannelEventInitDict);
  readonly attribute LP2PDataChannel channel;
};

dictionary LP2PDataChannelEventInit : EventInit {
    required LP2PDataChannel channel;
};
</pre>

Extensions Events {#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions-events}
------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PConnection}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PConnection"><code>datachannel</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannelEvent}}</td>
      <td>An incoming data channel is received.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Extensions Event handlers {#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions-event-handlers}
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the {{LP2PConnection}} interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection"><code>ondatachannel</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PConnection/datachannel}}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Examples {#data-channel-communication-examples}
---------------------------------------------------

Example: Receive a {{LP2PDataChannel}} on an existing connection as receiver:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});

receiver.onconnection = e => {
    const conn = e.connection;
    console.log("Receiver: Got a connection!");

    conn.ondatachannel = e => {
        const channel = e.channel;

        channel.onmessage = e => {
            const message = e.data;
            console.log(\`Receiver: Received message: ${message}\`);
        };

        channel.send("Good day to you, requester!");
    };
};

await receiver.start();
</pre>

Example: Create a {{LP2PDataChannel}} on an existing connection as requester:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

const conn = await request.start();
console.log("Requester: Got a connection!");

const channel = conn.createDataChannel("My Channel");

channel.onopen = e => {
    channel.onmessage = e => {
        const message = e.data;
        console.log(\`Requester: Received message: ${message}\`);
    };

    channel.send("Good day to you, receiver!");
};
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PQuicTransport">

The LP2PQuicTransport Interface {#lp2p-quic-transport}
======================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PQuicTransport</dfn> Interface allows opening a [[!webtransport]] between peers. If the LP2PRequest or LP2PReceiver is not yet started, it must be started when the LP2PQuicTransport is constructed.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PQuicTransport : WebTransport {
  constructor((LP2PRequest or LP2PReceiver) source,
              optional LP2PQuicTransportInit quicTransportDict = {});
};

dictionary LP2PQuicTransportInit {
};
</pre>

Issue: Define LP2PQuicTransportInit as needed using WebTransportOptions as reference.

LP2PQuicTransportListener Interface {#lp2p-quic-transport-listener}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The <dfn interface>LP2PQuicTransportListener</dfn> Interface allows listening for incoming {{LP2PQuicTransport}} transports. If the LP2PRequest or LP2PReceiver is not yet started, it must be started when the LP2PQuicTransportListener is constructed.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PQuicTransportListener {
  constructor((LP2PRequest or LP2PReceiver) source,
              optional LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit quicTransportListenerDict = {});
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined&gt; ready;
  /* a ReadableStream of LP2PQuicTransport objects */
  readonly attribute ReadableStream incomingTransports;
};

dictionary LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit {
};
</pre>

Issue: Define LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit as needed using WebTransportOptions as reference.

Examples {#lp2p-quic-transport-examples}
----------------------------------------

Example: Setting up a request for a LP2PQuicTransport:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(request);

// Blocks until transport is ready.
await transport.ready;
</pre>

Example: Receiving a LP2PQuicTransport:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});

const listener = new LP2PQuicTransportListener(receiver);

for await (const transport of listener.incomingTransports) {
  // Blocks until transport is ready.
  await transport.ready;
}
</pre>

Refer to the [WebTransport examples](https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#examples) for usage of a [[!webtransport]] object.

</section>

Security and privacy considerations {#security-and-privacy-considerations}
=========================================================================

Open Screen Network Protocol {#security-osp}
------------------------------------
The Local Peer-to-Peer API is meant to be implementable on the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]. The Security and Privacy considerations of the Open Screen Network Protocol must therefore be considered when implementing this API.

Personally identifiable information {#security-pii}
---------------------------------------------------
The peer attributes, such as the nickname and device model, are provided to give minimal context to a connected peer. This information could be used in conjunction with other information for fingerprinting the user. However, this information is only available to an origin after it has been authenticated and a user has given explicit consent to make the connection to the remote peer.

No information is exposed to the origin during service discovery. No IP information is exposed to an origin. This is fully managed by the user agent.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#15): Refine the peer attributes exposed to origins.

User interface guidelines {#security-ui}
----------------------------------------

When the user is asked to grant permission to connect to a peer, the user agent should make it clear what origin the request is coming from.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#15): Define peer filtering to give the user additional context.

Impact on same-origin policy {#security-same-origin}
----------------------------------------

This document extends the Web platform with the ability to set up real-time, direct communication between browsers and other devices, including other browsers, within a [=local communication medium=].

This means that data and media can be shared between applications running in different browsers, or between an application running in the browser and another user agent that is not a browser such as a headless service provided by a smart TV or smart fridge. This extends the usual barriers in the Web's security model that prevents sending data between entities with different origins.

Device Access {#security-device-access}
---------------------------------------

The Local Peer-to-Peer API requires user permission for a page to access any peers. The API uses purpose-fit protocols for data communication. It cannot be used to connect to raw sockets or unknowing HTTP servers. In addition, a user must [=acquire a local peer grant=] by explicitly providing consenting for an origin to connect to a peer. In addition, a user must [=authenticate a local peer=] before use.

Persistent State {#security-persistent-state}
---------------------------------------------
The persisted state considerations of the  [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] [Persistent State rules](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#persistent-state) must be followed when implementing this API.

Secure Contexts {#security-secure-context}
------------------------------------------
The Local Peer-to-Peer API must only be provided in a secure context.

Appendix A: OSP Extension Messages {#appendix-a}
================================================

The following messages are defined according to and as an extension to the [[!openscreen-application|Open Screen Application Protocol]] [Messages](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a). The `request`, `response` and `result` types are defined there.

Note: The type keys and capability IDs for these extensions are not officially registered yet. They will be registered as this specification matures. 

<pre class="data" highlight='cddl'>
agent-capability /= &(
  <dfn>data-channels</dfn>: 1100
  <dfn>quic-transport</dfn>: 1200
)

<dfn>data-channel-encoding-id</dfn> = &(
  <dfn>encoding-id-blob</dfn>: 0
  <dfn>encoding-id-string</dfn>: 1
  <dfn>encoding-id-array-buffer</dfn>: 2
)

; data-frame (type key 24) is the Open Screen Application Protocol message;
; only its encoding-id (0) and payload (4) keys are used.

; type key 1101
<dfn>data-channel-open-request</dfn> = {
  request
  1: uint ; channel-id
  2: text ; label
  3: text ; protocol
}

; type key 1102
<dfn>data-channel-open-response</dfn> = {
  response
  1: &amp;result ; result
}
</pre>